Open Questions for User
Notebook granularity: One notebook per claim (26+) vs per claim-cluster (6-8 domain notebooks) vs per visualization type?
Recommendation: Per-claim for traceability, but group tightly related claims (e.g., C043-C050 all in one flexibility comparison notebook)
Output format: Pure .ipynb committed to repo, or also export to HTML/Quarto for static site?
Recommendation: .ipynb in notebooks/ (gitignored outputs), with make export to generate HTML via jupyter nbconvert
Visualization libraries: Plotly (interactive, heavy), Altair (declarative, Vega-Lite), Matplotlib/Seaborn (static, light), PyVis (network graphs)?
Recommendation: Plotly for interactivity + Altair for grammar-of-graphics; PyVis only for network diagrams (border router, predicate graph)
Parameterization: Should notebooks accept parameters (e.g., claim_id) via papermill for batch rendering?
Recommendation: Yes — design core library so notebooks are thin wrappers; enables papermill batch runs
Scope: Include Extraction 1 (stage2.duckdb) comparisons, or smarthome only?
Recommendation: smarthome only for v1; cross-extraction comparison as separate phase
CI integration: Run notebooks headless in CI to verify they execute without error?
Recommendation: Add pytest --nbval or jupyter nbconvert --execute in CI pipeline
Validation Criteria
 Every notebook opens and executes without error (headless)
 Provenance panel shows correct Claim ID, Source IDs, Extraction Log refs
 Confidence/evidence class visually encoded (color, icon, tooltip)
 Uncertainty flags visible: [conditions not stated], [falsifier not stated], conflicting metrics
 Hover/tooltip shows source citation and raw value
 Notebook reads from data/smarthome.duckdb (read-only) — no external files
 Core library has unit tests (src/tests/test_viz_core.py)
 Index notebook links to all claim notebooks with search/filter
